WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.054
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.054 --> 00:00:12.810
For many years, I was focused on
health policy and I actually worked

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:17.240
with the Clinton administration
to try to pass health reform.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:19.678
And when a colleague of mine came and
said,

00:00:19.678 --> 00:00:22.673
what's the next important
thing you want to work on?

00:00:22.673 --> 00:00:23.890
What are the big problems?

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:27.160
I said, clearly,
care coordination is the big problem.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:31.840
And so I took it on and started doing
work around it and, like everybody else,

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.050
I came across my own horror story,
really, my father got ill,

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:41.330
he spent more time than we would have
liked in the medical care system,

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:45.860
going from doctor to doctor, going from
hospital to hospital, my siblings and

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:49.900
I did our very best to keep
a notebook with all the notes.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:54.220
And we would transmit the information
as best we could from doctor to doctor.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.270
And ultimately,

00:00:56.270 --> 00:01:00.510
I say that my father passed away
because of a care coordination failure.

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:05.110
So the problem that I care a lot about and
have focused a great deal of my research

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:09.070
on recently has been care coordination,
I call it integrated care.

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:12.870
The reason I call it integrated care is
because I feel like it goes beyond just

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.130
mere coordination.

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:17.350
I really feel like
integration is something

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:20.060
more holistic because it accounts for

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:27.160
all of the customization that's required
to care for a particular patient.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:30.997
So it is the communication and
it is the coordination but

00:01:30.997 --> 00:01:36.802
it's done on behalf of each individual
patient according to the patient's needs.

00:01:36.802 --> 00:01:42.473
[MUSIC]

00:01:42.473 --> 00:01:45.427
Traditionally, the system
has grown up in silos so

00:01:45.427 --> 00:01:49.210
doctors have trained alone
nurses have chained separately.

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:53.630
They're trained to optimise what it is
they do not so much to communicate with

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:57.900
each other and it's the between
the cracks where the problems occur.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.016
So information doesn't transmit.

00:02:00.016 --> 00:02:04.211
When information doesn't transmit,
if a patient's been in a hospital, and

00:02:04.211 --> 00:02:08.723
then they go home, if that information
about the hospitalization doesn't get back

00:02:08.723 --> 00:02:13.042
to the primary care provider, that patient
is at risk for not having a follow-up

00:02:13.042 --> 00:02:16.944
appointment is much more likely to
get readmitted back to the hospital.

00:02:16.944 --> 00:02:21.694
The primary care physician refers
a patient out for a screening exam.

00:02:21.694 --> 00:02:26.164
But doesn't get the information about the
result of that exam back that patient is

00:02:26.164 --> 00:02:28.902
at risk for
not having the appropriate follow up.

00:02:28.902 --> 00:02:33.447
[MUSIC]

00:02:33.447 --> 00:02:37.443
We have a traditional payment system
in the US were predominantly,

00:02:37.443 --> 00:02:40.540
providers are still paid on a fee for
service basis.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:44.330
That means, if they perform a service,
they get a fee for that service.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:49.210
That encourages providers to provide more
services, it does not encourage providers

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:52.850
to spend time coordinating care
on behalf of their patients.

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:58.100
And so, there are efforts to reform
that payment systems, in ways that

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:02.420
align the incentives with the kinds of
initiatives that I've been talking about.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:06.350
So rather than a strict fee for service,
there are experiments around, for

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:11.220
example, Accountable Care Organizations
and some Accountable Care Organizations

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:16.490
assume risk for either physician
services or for all services.

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:20.146
And you have more of an incentive to try
and create the kinds of processes and

00:03:20.146 --> 00:03:23.744
programs that I've been describing
that allow the providers to attend to

00:03:23.744 --> 00:03:25.906
the coordination needs of those patients.

00:03:25.906 --> 00:03:30.855
[MUSIC]

00:03:30.855 --> 00:03:34.355
Over decades, the VA has been
leader in terms of quality and

00:03:34.355 --> 00:03:38.345
efficiency of the care that they
deliver on behalf of the veterans,

00:03:38.345 --> 00:03:42.895
and the reason that they have been so far
ahead of the rest of the system in the US

00:03:42.895 --> 00:03:45.835
is because it's an integrated
delivery system.

00:03:45.835 --> 00:03:50.310
It's of one of the very few we have
examples of in the United States.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.798
The primary doctors and
the specialist and the hospitals,

00:03:52.798 --> 00:03:54.820
they're all part of the same system.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:59.071
And they have four decades shared
an electronic medical record system,

00:03:59.071 --> 00:04:03.877
which means that as a primary care doctor,
I could see what the specialist has done

00:04:03.877 --> 00:04:08.473
on behalf of my patient and I can follow
up in a way that in the private healthcare

00:04:08.473 --> 00:04:11.633
system, we very often don't
have the ability to do.

00:04:11.633 --> 00:04:16.603
So for those reasons, they have had great
advantages to be able to deliver high

00:04:16.603 --> 00:04:19.200
quality and more integrated care.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.450
And they have had their share of visionary
leaders who've really prioritized

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:27.200
quality and coordination and
patient centeredness.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.160
And when you have that
along with caring for

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:33.510
your patient, the combination of
those things are pretty powerful.

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:35.013
Everybody really wants to do their best.

00:04:35.013 --> 00:04:40.399
[MUSIC]

00:04:40.399 --> 00:04:45.116
They're instituting a new program
called their Care Coordination and

00:04:45.116 --> 00:04:47.719
Integrated Case Management Program.

00:04:47.719 --> 00:04:51.940
They're creating hierarchical
levels of intervention.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:57.590
So for the very most complex patients,
they have one set of processes.

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:02.370
So they're actually assigning a care
coordinator to work with that patient and

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:03.420
the family.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:06.820
To go with that patient to
their different visits, and

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:10.730
to make sure that the information flows
back to their primary care doctor.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:13.490
And for the next swath of patients,
still complex, but

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:17.040
a little bit less so, they have
another level of intervention, and

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:20.900
then for a level down from there,
third level of intervention.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:25.390
So they're trying to assign
their resources according to

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:27.409
the needs of the patient.

00:05:27.409 --> 00:05:30.323
[MUSIC]

00:05:30.323 --> 00:05:34.862
We've gone into practice sites to try
to understand what they're doing,

00:05:34.862 --> 00:05:39.689
what tools they're using to try to tease
out what's a better practice than then

00:05:39.689 --> 00:05:41.495
not a better practice.

00:05:41.495 --> 00:05:46.825
And interestingly, when we did this
initially over 150 practice sites,

00:05:46.825 --> 00:05:51.740
we couldn't discern anything
that was differentiating them,

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:57.670
the things that we expected would
be differentiating whether they had

00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:02.900
a shared electronic medical record system,
whether they were multidisciplinary,

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:05.460
whether they were sophisticated
in terms of their IT, or

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:09.130
sophisticated in terms of their
care management processes.

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:13.860
None of those things systematically
related to improving integration for

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:14.640
their patients.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:18.798
And so, we went to organizations
that the patients suggested were

00:06:18.798 --> 00:06:23.020
high-performing versus low-performing, to
try to tease out some of the differences.

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:26.340
And what we finally discerned
kind of at its essence

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:31.200
was that organizations could take
whatever resources they had available,

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:36.440
whether it be a sophisticated electronic
medical record system, or whether it was

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:42.620
a kind of individual resource like a care
management person within the organization.

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:44.808
So long as they took those resources and

00:06:44.808 --> 00:06:48.922
really focused their attention on
the goal which was to integrate care for

00:06:48.922 --> 00:06:52.794
the patients, there were lots of
different paths to getting there.

00:06:52.794 --> 00:06:58.542
[MUSIC]

00:06:58.542 --> 00:07:03.061
If somebody says I want to start, where
should I start, I always tell them to

00:07:03.061 --> 00:07:06.794
measure first to find out where
their unique strengths are and

00:07:06.794 --> 00:07:09.460
where their unique challenges are.

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:12.010
And once they have that,
they can really create for

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:15.230
themselves a roadmap that I
think makes sense because

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:18.140
every organization is going to be
a little bit different that way.

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:22.710
What we're looking at is, is trying to get
people to make incremental improvement.

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:26.310
There's a tremendous amount
of focus on this country.

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:31.424
The next great drug or the next
great device and instead my research

00:07:31.424 --> 00:07:36.992
focus is on kind of the next great
improvement in ways that we can transmit

00:07:36.992 --> 00:07:41.831
information and coordinate care
because if we don't do that,

00:07:41.831 --> 00:07:47.709
then the the drug is not going to produce
the benefit that we need it to produce.

00:07:47.709 --> 00:07:51.615
If we have a procedure because it's a
great new procedure that we're able to do

00:07:51.615 --> 00:07:55.877
with some sort of automated robotic way of
doing it, that's tremendous, but if there

00:07:55.877 --> 00:07:59.903
isn't appropriate follow up for that
patient, they're not going to benefit from

00:07:59.903 --> 00:08:04.655
having that procedure, and they can end up
either worse of or back into the hospital.

00:08:04.655 --> 00:08:10.555
And so, in order to make them work well,
we need cultures within organizations,

00:08:10.555 --> 00:08:15.775
and people within organizations
that prioritize their combinations.

00:08:15.775 --> 00:08:19.316
So it's going to be very incremental, but

00:08:19.316 --> 00:08:23.691
if we could actually move
the ball down the field,

00:08:23.691 --> 00:08:29.954
we'd be saving life after life after life,
it would be a game changer.

00:08:29.954 --> 00:08:33.114
[MUSIC]

